WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:06.800
The MCU is going where it never dared to go
before — specifically, to the Netflix shows.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.949
"That's it, let the Devil out."

00:00:09.949 --> 00:00:14.750
"Spider-Man: No Way Home" begins directly
after the events of "Spider-Man: Far From

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:18.610
Home," with Peter Parker on the run after
his identity is outed.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:22.680
Now on the run, Peter's life as he once knew
it is over.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:27.220
With safety hard to come by, Peter is desperate
to set things right, teaming up with Doctor

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.640
Strange to make the world forget he's Spider-Man.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.520
Naturally, hijinks ensue.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.980
In addition to bringing a whole lot of bad
guys — and good guys — in from other movie

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:41.570
universe, Peter's adventure also leads him
to a lawyer who might look pretty familiar

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:44.010
to fans of the Netflix Marvel series.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.810
That's right — it finally happened.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.440
"So the Devil is back."

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:53.450
During one scene in "No Way Home," Peter is
sitting at a table with Aunt May, Happy Hogan,

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:58.059
and his mysterious lawyer -- the sunglasses-wearing
attorney Matt Murdock.

00:00:58.059 --> 00:01:02.410
During the meeting, a brick gets thrown through
the window at blinding speed, with a note

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:06.270
attached to it from someone blaming Peter
for the death of Mysterio.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:10.869
Parker can barely react before the blind Murdock
reaches out and catches it with lightning

00:01:10.869 --> 00:01:13.530
reflexes, shocking even Peter himself.

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:16.490
When asked how exactly he did that, Murdock
responds,

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:19.009
"I'm a really good lawyer."

00:01:19.009 --> 00:01:24.390
With that, it's as confirmed as can possibly
be that Matt Murdock, Daredevil powers and

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.749
all, is now officially a part of the big-screen
MCU.

00:01:27.749 --> 00:01:32.920
"This change now, it feels significant."

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:37.130
He's never named directly in the scene -- it
is a proper cameo -- but the face of Charlie

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:40.039
Cox is unmistakable for Marvel fans.

00:01:40.039 --> 00:01:44.939
The actor of course played this very same
lawyer in Marvel's Netflix series "Daredevil"

00:01:44.939 --> 00:01:47.939
and the rest of the "Defenders"-associated
shows.

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:52.099
You might be thinking, "How do we know he's
the same version of the same character, not

00:01:52.099 --> 00:01:57.239
just a nod to the character, like how J.K.
Simmons plays two versions of J. Jonah Jameson

00:01:57.239 --> 00:01:58.570
that are clearly different people?"

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:00.349
"Mr. Jameson, there's a page six problem."

00:02:00.349 --> 00:02:02.619
"We have a page one problem — shut up."

00:02:02.619 --> 00:02:05.639
That's a fair question, and the answer remains
to be seen.

00:02:05.639 --> 00:02:10.130
Does this mean that "Daredevil" and the other
Netflix shows are now fully canonical in the

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:11.130
MCU?

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:15.670
At this point, it's hard to say — especially
after "Loki" introduced the idea of variants

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:18.250
to characters we know across dimensions.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:22.860
For all we know, Matt Murdock in "No Way Home"
and the Kingpin in "Hawkeye" might just be

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:27.280
the MCU versions of these characters, with
their Netflix adventures occurring in a slightly

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:28.560
different world.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.750
Either way, we can probably count on Matt
and Peter crossing paths again — eventually.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:37.690
Strange may have made everyone forget who
Peter is, but these characters have a comic

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:40.010
book history that's too big to ignore.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:44.820
As New York-based heroes with enhanced reflexes,
the pair have had a lot of interactions and

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.730
team-ups before in Marvel lore.

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:52.310
In "The Spectacular Spider-Man" issues #27
through #30, Peter Parker gets blinded and

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:54.920
is aided by Daredevil until his recovery.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:03:01.640
In "Daredevil" issues #305 and #306, the pair
team up to stop a vicious female serial killer.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:07.260
A grieving, violent Spider-Man nearly kills
a criminal in rage in "The Spectacular Spider-Man"

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:13.230
#110 until Daredevil distracts the hero long
enough for him to come to his senses.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:17.820
Their partnership is one of the stronger informal
team-ups in Marvel lore, so it's something

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:21.910
special to see Murdock finally get to help
out our favorite web-slinger.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:26.181
Any fan of the comics has a lot to be excited
about now that Daredevil and Spider-Man are

00:03:26.181 --> 00:03:29.470
finally united in the same cinematic universe.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:34.170
And plenty of fans are probably just happy
to see Charlie Cox as Matt Murdock again.

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:39.450
In last year's Looper poll, we found out that
out of all the major canceled Netflix series,

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:44.760
"Daredevil" was far-and-away the one that
a clear majority of fans wanted to return.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.610
The show grabbed a whopping 21% of all polled.

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:52.240
The show may still be off the air, but you've
got to admit — it looks like all those fans

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:54.160
just got their wish.

